(FIL-58, FIL-59, FIL-60, FIL-62: `Seek <file> to byte N` and its plural
 synonym `to bytes N` are both accepted (LANGUAGE.md:4032-4033), seek
 positions are 1-indexed so `byte 1` is file offset 0 (LANGUAGE.md:4037),
 and an arbitrary byte N moves to byte position N (LANGUAGE.md:4039). One
 file, two known halves - a prefix and a suffix, each a random-length
 filler the generator wrote - proves all four at once: seeking to byte 1
 and reading the prefix's own length back must return exactly the
 prefix (1-indexing), and seeking to the byte right after the prefix and
 reading the suffix's length must return exactly the suffix (arbitrary
 offset). Each seek draws its own keyword ("byte"/"bytes")
 independently, so both spellings appear across the corpus without
 either being fixed to one half. `Seek` was emitted by no leaf anywhere
 in `src/` before this batch - a documented invariant defect this
 ledger's own *Invariants* section names. Probe: docs/ledger/probes/
 files/FIL-57.vox (re-hand-verified this batch under both compilers).)

