(FIL-89: all resources are cleaned up on exit even when you forget to
 `Close` (LANGUAGE.md:4172-4180, the manual's own worked example).
 `Write` is unbuffered (FIL-01/FIL-56's own leaves already lean on this),
 so a second, independent handle opened on the same path BEFORE the
 writer is ever closed already sees every byte - proof the bytes reached
 disk without a `Close`, from inside the program itself, with no
 dependence on what happens at exit (which nothing inside Vox can
 observe - FIL-95). The writer is deliberately left open for the rest of
 the program; that is the claim under test, not an oversight.
 Incidentally touches the same "no manual cleanup needed" thesis
 FIL-84/FIL-88/FIL-98/FIL-99 restate in different words, though this
 leaf is scoped to FIL-89 alone. Probe: docs/ledger/probes/files/
 FIL-89.vox.)

