(FIL-21: an out-of-bounds list read sets the error flag and yields a
 default - LANGUAGE.md:3916-3921. Where the compiler can prove the index
 is past the end it yields the NUMBER 0, so only a number holder is
 legal; where it cannot, it yields the holder's own default: 0, the empty
 text, [] or {}. Hand-verified (docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-21.vox):

 - provable: literal 0 and any positive literal past the end, i64's top
   included - a text holder there is a compile error, so the literal
   forms are only ever drawn for a list of numbers;
 - not provable: a variable index, AND a negative literal - the compiler
   does not treat `element -2 of` as provably out of range, so a text
   holder compiles and reads the empty text. That matches the manual's
   "where the compiler can prove" wording and is not recorded as a
   discrepancy, but it is why a negative literal is drawn here only for
   numbers too: a compiler that started proving it would turn a text
   holder into a compile failure, which the fuzzer must never emit.

 The element type is drawn - numbers, texts, lists or maps - and every
 non-number type reads through a variable index holding 0, a negative, a
 position just past the end, or a 'gen extreme number' past it. The
 stale-flag drain from 'gen leaf read line mechanics' runs first, so a
 flag left by an earlier leaf is never read as this one's. This covers
 the collections ledger's LST2-25/LST2-28 territory too; the row named
 here is the File I/O chapter's restatement of it.)

