(FIL-39: opening for writing creates OR OVERWRITES - LANGUAGE.md:3984 -
 so reopening an existing file for writing truncates it. A long payload
 is written and closed; the reopen must read size 0 on the live handle
 before anything is written, then exactly the short payload's length
 after it, and reading the file back must give the short payload alone,
 with none of the long one's tail. The short payload is drawn strictly
 shorter, down to zero bytes, so a reopen that merely overwrote in place
 without truncating leaves a tail the content check sees.

 After the short write the handle is closed and written to AGAIN: a
 write on a closed handle is a failure the manual says sets the error
 flag (LANGUAGE.md:4077-4079, the Writing rules; FIL-80's catchable file
 operations), and a write through a closed descriptor is exactly the
 use-after-close the memory-safety promise is about. Probe:
 docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-39.vox.)

