    a text called 'the read close' is "Close {'the handle'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the path line', 'the long open', 'the long write', 'the long check', 'the long close', 'the short open', 'the truncated check', 'the short write', 'the short check', 'the short close', 'the stale declaration', 'the stale handler', 'the late declaration', 'the late write', 'the late handler', 'the late check', 'the read open', 'the sink line', 'the read line', 'the content capture', 'the content check', 'the read close'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(FIL-36, FIL-43: a numeric `at` is a borrowed descriptor, not a path
 (LANGUAGE.md:3972), and descriptor literals run 0..2147483647
 (LANGUAGE.md:3990). The oracle for "borrowed" is the handle's own
 `descriptor`: a path open gets a fresh descriptor of 3 or more, a
 borrowed one reports exactly the number written. Asking whether a file
 named "1" appeared would test the harness's working directory, which
 the program does not own, so that half is not emitted.

 One to three borrowed opens, each drawn from the targets PROCEDURE.md
 6's allowlist and the earlier leaves already use:
 - descriptor 1 for writing - stdout; a short marker line is sometimes
   written through it, which lands in the program's own output;
 - descriptor 0 for reading - only when the harness feeds this program
   stdin, the same condition 'gen leaf stdin read' opens it under, and it
   is never read here, so a later stdin leaf still sees all of its input;
 - descriptor 2147483647, the top of FIL-43's range, in a drawn mode: it
   is never an open descriptor, so the open itself succeeds (hand-
   verified) and the first real operation on it - a write, or a read
   into a buffer that must stay empty - fails and must set the error flag
   (LANGUAGE.md:4139-4141, FIL-80).
 Descriptor 2 is never opened (generated programs never touch stderr).
 Neither 0 nor 1 is ever closed: closing a borrowed descriptor closes
 the process's own stdin or stdout - harness.vox's own child setup
 relies on exactly that - and every later Print would then fail. No
 path is built, so the leaf needs no scratch directory. Probe:
 docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-36.vox.)

To 'gen leaf file borrowed descriptors' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a list called 'the lines' is [].
    a number called 'the open count draw' is 'rng below' of 3.
    a number called 'the open count' is 'the open count draw' add 1.
    a number called 'the opened' is 0.
    While 'the opened' is less than 'the open count',
        a text called 'the stem' is 'gen buffer name',
        a number called 'the target draw' is 'rng below' of 3,
        If 'the target draw' is 2 and gen_reads_stdin is false then,
            Set 'the target draw' to 'rng below' of 2.
        a list called 'the borrowed block' is [],
        If 'the target draw' is 0 then,
            Set 'the borrowed block' to 'gen file borrow the top descriptor' of 'the stem'.
        If 'the target draw' is 1 then,
            Set 'the borrowed block' to 'gen file borrow standard output' of 'the stem'.
        If 'the target draw' is 2 then,
            Set 'the borrowed block' to 'gen file borrow standard input' of 'the stem'.
        Set 'the lines' to 'gen concat two blocks' of 'the lines' and 'the borrowed block',
        Set 'the opened' to 'the opened' add 1.
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Descriptor 1, for writing, sometimes with a marker line written through
 it - the borrowed write lands in the program's own stdout.)

To 'gen file borrow standard output' with a text called 'the stem'.
    a text called 'the handle' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the stem'.
    a text called 'the descriptor access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "descriptor".
    a list called 'the block' is [].
    a text called 'the open line' is 'gen file open in any order' of "writing" and 'the handle' and "1".
    a text called 'the descriptor check' is 'gen assert number is' of "FIL-36" and 'the descriptor access' and 1.
    append 'the open line' to 'the block'.
    append 'the descriptor check' to 'the block'.
    a number called 'the marker draw' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a number called 'the marker length draw' is 'rng below' of 20.
    a number called 'the marker length' is 'the marker length draw' add 1.
    a text called 'the marker' is 'gen buffer filler' of 'the marker length'.
    If 'the marker draw' is 1 then,
        append "Write \"{'the marker'}\\n\" to {'the handle'}" to 'the block'.
    Return a list, 'the block'.

(Descriptor 0, for reading, never read - see the leaf's comment.)

To 'gen file borrow standard input' with a text called 'the stem'.
    a text called 'the handle' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the stem'.
    a text called 'the descriptor access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "descriptor".
    a list called 'the block' is [].
    a text called 'the open line' is 'gen file open in any order' of "reading" and 'the handle' and "0".
    a text called 'the descriptor check' is 'gen assert number is' of "FIL-43" and 'the descriptor access' and 0.
    append 'the open line' to 'the block'.
    append 'the descriptor check' to 'the block'.
    Return a list, 'the block'.

(Descriptor 2147483647 in a drawn mode, then one operation on it - a
 read into a buffer of drawn capacity, or a write of a drawn payload -
 that must set the error flag, behind the usual stale-flag drain.)

To 'gen file borrow the top descriptor' with a text called 'the stem'.
    a text called 'the handle' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the stem'.
    a text called 'the descriptor access' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the stem' and "descriptor".
    a list called 'the block' is [].
    a number called 'the mode draw' is 'rng below' of 3.
    a text called 'the mode' is "reading".
    If 'the mode draw' is 1 then,
        Set 'the mode' to "writing".
    If 'the mode draw' is 2 then,
        Set 'the mode' to "appending".
    a text called 'the open line' is 'gen file open in any order' of 'the mode' and 'the handle' and "2147483647".
    a text called 'the descriptor check' is 'gen assert number is' of "FIL-43" and 'the descriptor access' and 2147483647.
    append 'the open line' to 'the block'.
    append 'the descriptor check' to 'the block'.
    a text called 'the stale flag' is 'gen buffer companion' of "found an error already pending".
    append "a boolean called {'the stale flag'} is false" to 'the block'.
    append "On error Set {'the stale flag'} to true" to 'the block'.
    a text called 'the failure flag' is 'gen buffer companion' of "dead descriptor refused".
    append "a boolean called {'the failure flag'} is false" to 'the block'.
    a text called 'the sink stem' is 'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the sink name' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the sink stem'.
    a number called 'the capacity' is 'gen buffer size'.
    a number called 'the payload length draw' is 'rng below' of 30.
    a number called 'the payload length' is 'the payload length draw' add 1.
    a text called 'the payload' is 'gen buffer filler' of 'the payload length'.
    If 'the mode draw' is 0 then,
        append "a buffer called {'the sink name'} is {'the capacity'} bytes in size" to 'the block',
        append "Read from {'the handle'} into {'the sink name'}" to 'the block'.
    If 'the mode draw' is greater than 0 then,
        append "Write \"{'the payload'}\" to {'the handle'}" to 'the block'.
    append "On error Set {'the failure flag'} to true" to 'the block'.
    append 'gen assert true' of "FIL-80" and 'the failure flag' to 'the block'.
    Return a list, 'the block'.

(Scratch view for measuring a text's length in bytes, program level
 and 'clear'ed on each use for the same Defect 17 reason as 'the built
 input' above: a text has no size property, and a per-call buffer would
 be one 4 KB allocation per call, never freed.)

a buffer called 'the byte measuring view' is "".

(FIL-48: `Read from standard input into <buffer>` - the manual's own
 spelling, LANGUAGE.md:4009 and the keyword table at 5293. 'gen leaf
 stdin read' reaches stdin only through "/dev/stdin" or descriptor 0.

 The generator wrote the input, but not which reads run first: another
 stdin leaf, a loop or a function called twice can each consume some of
 it before this one. So the assertion is a bound, not a value: every
 read lands no more than the input's length, a fixed buffer no more than
 its capacity, and two reads together no more than the input - an
 agreement check across the reads, the PROCEDURE.md 6a pattern for an
 input the generator does not fully control.

 The overflow flag is caught and printed but NOT asserted. A fixed
 buffer smaller than the waiting input is a "Fixed buffer overflow (data
 exceeds capacity)" by LANGUAGE.md:4138, and `Read from <file>` sets the
 flag there, but `Read from standard input` does not, on a pipe or on a
 redirected file alike - an open discrepancy with the manual, not
 encoded either way. Probe: docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-48.vox.)

To 'gen leaf standard input read' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    If gen_reads_stdin is false then,
        'gen leaf print' of indent,
        Return a boolean, false.
    Set gen_stdin_reads to gen_stdin_reads add 1.
    clear 'the byte measuring view'.
    append gen_input to 'the byte measuring view'.
    a number called 'the input length' is 'the byte measuring view's size.
    a text called 'the input length shown' is "{'the input length'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [].
    a text called 'the total holder' is "".
    a number called 'the read count draw' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a number called 'the read count' is 'the read count draw' add 1.
    a number called 'the reads made' is 0.
    While 'the reads made' is less than 'the read count',
        a text called 'the sink stem' is 'gen buffer name',
        a text called 'the sink name' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'the sink stem',
        a text called 'the sink size' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the sink stem' and "size",
        a text called 'the overflow flag' is 'gen buffer companion' of "read flagged",
        a number called 'the capacity draw' is 'rng below' of 64,
        a number called 'the capacity' is 'the capacity draw' add 1,
        a number called 'the fixed draw' is 'rng below' of 3,
        a text called 'the sink line' is "a buffer called {'the sink name'} is {'the capacity'} bytes in size",
        If 'the fixed draw' is 0 then,
            Set 'the sink line' to 'gen dynamic buffer declaration' of 'the sink name'.
        append 'the sink line' to 'the lines',
        append "a boolean called {'the overflow flag'} is false" to 'the lines',
        append "Read from standard input into {'the sink name'}" to 'the lines',
        append "On error Set {'the overflow flag'} to true" to 'the lines',
        append "Print {'the overflow flag'}" to 'the lines',
        append 'gen assert time at most' of "FIL-48" and 'the sink size' and 'the input length shown' and 'the input length shown' to 'the lines',
        If 'the fixed draw' is greater than 0 then,
            append 'gen assert time at most' of "FIL-48" and 'the sink size' and "{'the capacity'}" and "{'the capacity'}" to 'the lines'.
        If 'the reads made' is 0 then,
            Set 'the total holder' to 'gen buffer companion' of "bytes taken from standard input",
            append "a number called {'the total holder'} is {'the sink size'}" to 'the lines'.
        If 'the reads made' is greater than 0 then,
            append "Set {'the total holder'} to {'the total holder'} add {'the sink size'}" to 'the lines'.
        Set 'the reads made' to 'the reads made' add 1.
    append 'gen assert time at most' of "FIL-48" and 'the total holder' and 'the input length shown' and 'the input length shown' to 'the lines'.
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(FIL-50: `Read line from <file handle> into <buffer>` reads one line of a
 file - LANGUAGE.md:4016. Batch B2's 'gen leaf read line mechanics' reads
 two lines of a fixed shape; this walks a whole file of one to eight
 lines line by line, each line a drawn length from zero - so empty lines,
 "\n" alone, come back as one-byte reads - with the last line's newline
 drawn (present or absent), into a sink that is fixed with room to spare
 or dynamic. Every read is checked twice: its size (the line plus one
 for a kept newline, LANGUAGE.md:4021) and its exact bytes. One read past
 the last line must come back empty (LANGUAGE.md:4022, FIL-54). Lines
 hold only 'gen buffer filler' letters and digits, so the expected text
 needs no escaping beyond its own newline. Probe:
 docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-50.vox.)

